WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:31.175
For decades the direction seemed clear: young people moved to the cities, where the jobs, universities and culture were, while many villages aged and schools, doctors' practices and shops had to close. For some years, however, reports of a counter-movement have been multiplying. Families are buying empty farmsteads, creative people are setting up shared offices in old railway stations, and some villages that seemed long written off are gaining new residents for the first time.

00:00:31.425 --> 00:00:54.913
What made this possible above all is digitalisation. Anyone who can do their job on a laptop is no longer necessarily tied to one place — provided the internet is fast enough. On top of that come the steeply risen rents in the big conurbations: for the price of a three-room flat in Munich, in some regions you can get a whole house with a garden.

00:00:55.163 --> 00:01:28.937
But the idyll has its snags. Anyone who moves to the countryside often depends on a car, because in many places buses run only twice a day. The nearest paediatrician may be half an hour away, and the fibre-optic cable promised in the brochure is slow to arrive. And different ways of life often collide: long-standing residents sometimes see the newcomers as city people who romanticise the village but join neither the fire brigade nor the sports club.

00:01:29.187 --> 00:01:54.770
There is also a question of fairness. So far, leaving the city is mainly a phenomenon of well-paid graduates whose work can move into a home office. The care worker, the bus driver or the shop assistant have no such choice — they have to live where their work is, and in the cities they pay the rents that others escape by moving to the country.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:02:23.959
Whether the movement becomes a lasting trend therefore depends less on the newcomers than on politics. Without reliable transport links, fast internet and basic medical care, country life remains a privilege for those who can afford to be flexible. So the question is not whether people want to move to the countryside, but whether they are given a life there that is more than a nice view.
